{:edb/db-before-t 1454 :edb/db-after-t 1455 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534767 50 #inst "2026-10-12T11:23:14.591Z" 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186171327 1078 #edb/ref 17592186171330 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186171339 1078 #edb/ref 17592186171340 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186171435 1078 #edb/ref 17592186171438 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186172005 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435504 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186172006 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435502 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186172007 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435500 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186172008 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435501 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186172008 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435503 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186172617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186172617 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\n1. The amount of time until the next earthquake in a particular country\n2. The number of giraffes in a zoo\n3. The number of defective bottles produced in a factory in a single day" 13194139534767 17592186045417 false] [17592186172617 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The amount of time until the next earthquake in a particular country\n\nII. The number of giraffes in a zoo\n\nIII. The number of defective bottles produced in a factory in a single day" 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186172617 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Earthquakes can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next earthquake occurs.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of continuous time or space.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of defective bottles produced is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186172619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186172619 1074 "Since the earthquakes occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next earthquake using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next earthquake. Since there is an earthquake every $2.5$ months on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2.5}=2/5$ earthquakes per month.\n\nThe number of earthquakes can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =2/5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{2}{5}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next earthquake will not occur for at least $6$ months is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-2x/5}|}_{6}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ={e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ≈0.0907.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186172635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186172635 1074 "Since the errors occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next error using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next error. The time until the next error can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since errors occur once every $30$ days on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/30,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{30}).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that first error will be registered between $2$ and $12$ days is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<12) & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/30}{|}_{2}^{12} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/5}+{e}^{-1/15} \\\\  & ≈0.2652\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186435478 1080 "0.982" 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186435478 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186435478 1083 #uuid "105e844e-764d-56e7-b3ce-44d005a43128" 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186435479 1080 "0.2337" 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186435479 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186435479 1083 #uuid "147b0fac-812b-5ffc-b68e-0bb7f6a18d22" 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186435480 1080 "0.393" 13194139534767 17592186045417 true] [17592186435480 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534767 17592186